  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nMidday surface observations clearly defined the cold front\nbisecting SW KS, displaying density current tendencies. The cold\nfront was making good southward progress, already reaching\nDumas, Texas as of 11am, but the cold front had only reached\nGCK at the same time. The boundary will continue to sag slowly\nsoutheast this afternoon, slowing down some as it encounters\nincreasingly hotter air ahead of it. This process will create \na large temperature range across the DDC CWA for midsummer \nstandards, ranging from lower 80s northwest, to near 105 near \nKiowa, Barber county. Elevated north winds will spread southward\nthis afternoon and evening, with gusts of 30-40 mph expected. \nRadar showed scattered light showers or sprinkles along and near\nthe cold front, and kept low pops in this afternoon for this \nactivity. \n\nA shortwave arriving from the Colorado Rockies tonight may\ngenerate some scattered rain showers with limited thunder \ntonight, favoring the northern zones, as shown by the latest \nNBM pop grids. In the post frontal environment, temperatures\nwill fall easily through the 60s overnight. Widespread\npost-frontal stratus is also expected, favoring areas along \nand north of US 50 through sunrise Wednesday. \n\nA challenging temperature forecast for Wednesday afternoon, with\nmodel consensus placing the stalled out frontal boundary near or\njust north of the KS/OK border. Forecast grids will carry only\nupper 70s along I-70, ranging to the upper 90s adjacent to\nOklahoma. Confident this gradient will exist, but precise\ntemperatures in between such as DDC are more difficult. \n12z MET forecasts a high of 78 at DDC Wednesday, and 12z MAV\nforecasts 91. A forecast in the 80s for most locales is\nappropriate, and will be a nice break from the heat. \nNAM is usually too cold, but if stratus can hold most of \nthe day, the colder solutions would verify. \n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms are anticipated Wednesday\nnight, amid NWly flow aloft, and the frontal boundary returning\nnorthward as a warm front with improved moisture. Much more\nuniform south/southeast winds are forecast Thursday afternoon \nas the warm front lifts north, with more uniform afternoon\ntemperatures in the 90s. One last opportunity for scattered\nthunderstorms Thursday night, with NBM pops favoring eastern\nzones with support from the 12z GFS. A shortwave rounding the\nnortheast periphery of the summer subtropical ridge may help\nstorms organized into a complex, and Thursday night is the best\nwindow for strong to marginally severe storms favoring central\nKansas. \n\nFriday through next Tuesday, the forecast returns to dry with \nanother several day heatwave expected, as influence from the \nsummer heat dome ridge increases. NBM afternoon temperature \ngrids suggest 97-103 will be common each afternoon, but barring \nsignificant rainfall between now and then, the NBM is probably \nnot hot enough in the extended.",
